📝 Main Topics
1. Account Bootstrap, Infrastructure Prerequisites, and Secret Rotation
Background:
The deployment requires account-level resources before any environment pipeline can run. The documented procedure is in enterprise/docs/MFA deployment.md; the manual authentication-related first step is not part of the required bootstrap flow.
Key Conclusion/Resolution:
Account bootstrap is the first CloudFormation stage and runs once per AWS account. It creates one S3 bucket for the CA file, copies the CA from the metadata location, and creates the role ARN referenced by environment configuration. The static Central CA KMS key provides access to and encryption for the CA in the metadata bucket.
The bootstrap also requires the target account permission boundary, application-owner and ABAC tags, and plaintext Docker, PIP, UV, and NPM configuration values in Secrets Manager for Artifactory and registry access. The resulting account must be validated before environment deployment proceeds.
Secret rotation is separate from bootstrap and is handled once per account. The implementation reads the NHID-related secret and updates plaintext configuration files, including Docker, PIP, UV, and NPM or NPM RC content. Docker and NHID rotate independently, so their schedules must be synchronized. The rotation implementation had not yet been tested.
